            <ArticleTitle>[Changes in small airway function in rhinitis without asthma].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1864-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Observe the changes of small airway function in patients with rhinitis but without asthma and/or lower airway symptoms.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Between June 2008 and December 2012, we recruited 903 subjects, including 377 with allergic rhinitis (AR), 262 with non-allergic rhinitis (NAR) and 264 healthy subjects. All subjects underwent meticulous history taking, nasal examination, allergen skin prick test, blood routine test, serum total immunoglobin E assay, pulmonary ventilation function test and bronchial challenge test.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULT" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The indices of FEV1/FVC%, MEF25pred% and MMEFpred% were lower in AR group than in the control group (P &lt; 0.05). The indices of FEV1/FVC, MMEFpred%, MEF25pred% and MEF50pred% were also lower in NAR group than in the control group (P &lt; 0.05). According to the FVCpred% and FEV1pred%, there were no differences between rhinitis group and the control group (P &gt; 0.05). The positive rate of airway hyperresponsiveness(AHR) in AR group and in NAR group was 12.2%, 6.1% respectively. Indices of small airway function were all lower in the AHR group than NAHR group in rhinitis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Compared with healthy controls, small airway function in patients with rhinitis has apparent changes, part of rhinitis patients has AHR, and is associated with small airway function changes.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
